# Security Rules

This prototype is automatically deployed to GitHub Pages on every push.
Generated code is visible to all organization members. Secrets detection
runs at three layers: agent (this rule), local (`scripts/scan-secrets.sh`),
and CI (gitleaks in the CircleCI pipeline). Prevention is better than detection.

## Prohibited Content

NEVER include any of the following in generated code:

- AWS access keys, secret keys, or session tokens
- API keys, tokens, or passwords (real or placeholder-looking, e.g. `sk-...`, `AKIA...`)
- Database connection strings or DSNs
- Private keys (RSA, SSH, PGP, EC)
- `.env` files with credential values
- Hardcoded URLs pointing to internal infrastructure (VPNs, private IPs, internal domains)
- JWTs, bearer tokens, or session cookies
- Basic auth credentials (usernames and passwords for real systems)

## Required Practices

- All API interactions MUST use MSW mock handlers in `src/mocks/handlers.ts`
- Use placeholder/fake data only (e.g., `user@example.com`, `Jane Doe`, `555-0100`)
- Never fetch from real API endpoints -- MSW intercepts all requests
- If the user asks to connect to a real API, explain that prototypes use mock data only and add a corresponding MSW handler instead
- Environment-specific configuration belongs in `.env` files listed in `.gitignore`, never committed

## Pre-commit Hooks

A pre-commit hook runs automatically on every `git commit`. It runs two checks:
1. **Type check** (`vue-tsc --build`) -- catches type errors before they reach CI
2. **Secrets scan** (gitleaks via `scripts/scan-secrets.sh`) -- blocks commits containing credentials

The hook is installed automatically by `npm install` (via the `prepare` script).
You do not need to run the hook or vue-tsc manually.
If it reports findings, fix the issues before committing. If absolutely necessary,
the hook can be bypassed with `git commit --no-verify`, but CI will still block
any secrets from being deployed.
